00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.700
BRUNO AZIZA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.533
This is Bruno Aziza,
and I'm here with Shan,

00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:05.370
and he is the brain behind
the anomaly detection design

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.950
pattern that we are
launching this week.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.250
If you saw our earlier
videos, these design patterns

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.380
are designed to
help you get started

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.260
and develop a solution to a
business problem you have.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.740
So Shan, thank you so much for
taking the time with us today.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.350
Tell us, first of all,
what is anomaly detection

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.670
and why does it matter?

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:24.770
SHAN KULANDAIVEL: Thanks
for having me, Bruno.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:25.830
I appreciate it.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.455
First, I need to let
you know that I've

00:00:27.455 --> 00:00:29.250
had three cups of
coffee today in the hope

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.980
that I can show up here with
at least half as much energy

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.210
as you do, but I don't
think it's going to cut it.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.043
I need to figure what
you have for breakfast

00:00:36.043 --> 00:00:38.785
and have that for breakfast,
lunch, and dinner.

00:00:38.785 --> 00:00:40.160
But anyway, with
that, let's talk

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.750
about anomaly detection, right?

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.060
Let me ask you this question.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.810
Let's assume this is
a regular working day

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.640
and you have to drive
in to work, right?

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.620
You probably saw hundreds and
hundreds of cars on the road.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.378
What are the one or two cars
that you're going to remember?

00:00:53.378 --> 00:00:54.670
BRUNO AZIZA: Yeah, that's true.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.045
I mean, my commute was actually
pretty easy this morning.

00:00:57.045 --> 00:00:59.520
But you're right, if I
drive to work, I don't know.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.380
I'll probably remember
a couple of cars.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.110
Maybe I saw three red cars?

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.355
But apart from that--

00:01:05.355 --> 00:01:05.670
SHAN KULANDAIVEL: Yeah, maybe--

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.210
BRUNO AZIZA: --I don't know.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.430
SHAN KULANDAIVEL: --one with
a flashy paint or maybe one

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.472
that was outsized, right?

00:01:09.472 --> 00:01:12.680
The small, on the smaller
side, or a large car, right?

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.220
Let me give you
another scenario.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.070
I've got an 18-month
toddler at home.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.140
She's a bundle of energy, always
running around doing things,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.560
asking for things.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.150
And she's around us, right?

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.660
So she stays in the
background, and then we

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.340
go on with our life.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.110
But the minute that
she's out of sight

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.770
and we don't hear
anything from her,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.250
that's when we know she's
up to something, right?

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.160
She's probably getting ready to
test out whether electrocution

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.483
is fake news or not.

00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:38.150
Like, she's probably
grabbing something,

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.790
trying to insert it
into the power socket.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.100
Now, what is common between
these two scenarios?

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.890
It is the fact that you've got
a constant stream of data coming

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.350
at you, but the data
that is outlier,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.720
or that is unlike the
others, is the one

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.310
that is more [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.930
It gives you
valuable perspective,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.180
tells you something
new, and, in my case,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.730
potentially helps
me avoid danger.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.820
Now, that's basically what
anomaly detection is, right?

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.060
Now, beyond those simple,
straightforward examples,

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.430
there's a lot of applications
for our customers.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.419
Let me give you a few examples.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.590
Let's say you're an
e-commerce provider.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.760
Monitoring your
transaction data,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.335
monitoring your card data,
monitoring your [? auto ?]

00:02:17.335 --> 00:02:18.960
information, your
monetary information,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.460
real-time [INAUDIBLE],,
you're going

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.440
to be able to detect
fraud much better

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.570
and prevent fraudulent
activities before it's

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.710
too late.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:26.730
Here is another example.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.590
Let's say you're a
manufacturing company, right?

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.465
You've got hundreds and
hundreds of equipment

00:02:30.465 --> 00:02:32.200
in your factory floor.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.280
Now being able to
analyze sensor data that

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.440
is coming from those
equipments in real time

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.650
and being able to detect
anomalous patterns

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.600
means you're able to schedule
proactive maintenance for maybe

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.800
equipment that
needs maintenance.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.420
Or you can pull out equipment
from your factory floor

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.918
before it's too late.

00:02:49.918 --> 00:02:51.460
Always, you are
going to be able to--

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.543
you're going to be dealing
with expensive repairs.

00:02:53.543 --> 00:02:55.080
Or even, in some
cases, you'll have

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.540
to shut down your
entire factory floor,

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:58.885
leading to significant costs.

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:00.760
BRUNO AZIZA: We talked
about e-commerce here.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.010
We talked about manufacturing.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.870
But, of course,
anomaly detection

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.900
is appropriate in
financial services,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.110
in telecommunications.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.710
So many of the folks
listening to us

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.150
will be able to apply that
for their business, really,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.380
regardless what industry.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.690
So you wrote a
great detailed blog

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.540
on the techniques being
used and how people

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.920
can take advantage of it.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.480
Take us through some of them.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.105
SHAN KULANDAIVEL:
We've published three

00:03:23.105 --> 00:03:24.720
different patents as of today.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.440
So the first one, we worked with
the telecommunications customer

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.280
to build a network log-based
anomaly detection--

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.070
our intrusion detection system.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.450
So this customer had a
rule-based system in place,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:37.950
right?

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.800
So rule-based
systems are when you

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.620
know of a specific pattern or
a [INAUDIBLE] or an intrusion,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.750
and then you codified
that and used this, right?

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.150
That works great, but
the significant drawback

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.940
of that is it can only
detect known packets, right?

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.880
Now, we all know the dynamic
world that we live in, right?

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.820
There are new forms of internet
code attacks every day.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.180
It's a constantly
evolving landscape.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.080
And so we work with the customer
to implement AI technique

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:04.590
called [INAUDIBLE] clustering.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.255
It's basically unsupervised
[? knowledge ?] technique

00:04:07.255 --> 00:04:10.770
to implement in our
network detection system

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.590
based on their NetFlow logs.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.960
Let me give you another example.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.250
So we work with a customer who
is basically a SAAS platform.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.620
Their additional trust and
safety platform basically

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.490
provides anomaly detection as
a service for their customers,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.560
who are mostly in
financial services,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.850
and e-commerce, and
digital commerce leaders.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.070
So we-- that customer was
able to [? liberate ?] some

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.590
of the patterns that we
publish to basically get up

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.698
and running, roll out
a production system.

00:04:39.698 --> 00:04:41.490
And in fact, they were
able to basically go

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.600
to market and scale and
meet the needs of some

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.110
of their very large customers.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.720
And their customers are
some of the biggest names

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.610
that you would think of when you
think about digital commerce.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.330
So what we are
seeing is customers

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.690
are now able to start, not
necessarily from the ground

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:58.200
floor--

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.450
they've already got the
scaffolding in place.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.340
They've got a system
that is architected

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.470
based on best practices.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.540
They're able to bring their
knowledge of their business,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.893
their domain, and really
finetune the system so

00:05:09.893 --> 00:05:11.310
that they are able
to better serve

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.518
the needs of their customers.

00:05:12.518 --> 00:05:15.300
At the end, that's what we want
customers to be able to do,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:15.840
right?

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.850
It doesn't make sense
for every single customer

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.930
to cobble together a system,
spend a lot of energy building

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:22.860
out a system.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.360
They should really
be focused on how

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.860
do they bring their knowledge
of their business, things that

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.820
are unique to the business,
and apply that to the patterns

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.325
that we provide so that they
are able to see value really,

00:05:32.325 --> 00:05:33.207
really quickly.

00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:34.290
BRUNO AZIZA: That's right.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.730
Well, Shan, thank you so much
for taking the time with us

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:37.230
